WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.604 --> 00:00:04.854
(gentle upbeat music)

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:17.760
- The world's most pressing
issues demand our attention,

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.760
and innovation is a way for
us to think outside of the box

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:24.720
and come up with solutions
in a way that is faster,

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.960
more efficient, and allows
us to solve problems

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.362
with greater success.

00:00:30.362 --> 00:00:32.460
YSPH is really unique

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:35.550
in that it's one of the only
schools of public health

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:39.690
that has a dedicated
program for innovation.

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:44.310
So InnovateHealth Yale was
started in 2013 at YSPH,

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:48.270
and it's a way to really
provide students, alumni,

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:52.170
and faculty who are
interested in challenging

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:54.840
and changing public health issues.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:58.590
And we're part of this vibrant
Yale innovation ecosystem

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:01.230
that has partners from across campus,

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:03.810
the School of Management, Yale Ventures,

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:06.180
and the Tsai Center for
Innovative Thinking,

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.325
as well as program partners

00:01:08.325 --> 00:01:10.350
at the School of the Environment

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:13.350
and the Graduate School
of Arts and Sciences.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:15.210
And it's a very supportive environment

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:18.060
because we have funding, mentorship,

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:22.320
and a collaborative community
across Yale University

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.410
to bounce ideas off of, meet partners.

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:28.890
And that's really how we come
up with a lot of our creative

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:30.300
and innovative solutions.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:32.400
InnovateHealth Yale has been involved

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.670
in so many interesting and
groundbreaking innovations,

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:37.860
and I'm happy to provide some examples.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:42.660
So KovaDx was the winner of
the 2020 Rita Wilson Prize

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.360
that was supported by InnovateHealth Yale,

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:50.040
and they use machine learning
to analyze red blood cells

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:52.200
to help screen for disease.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.780
And they've had a lot
of interesting impacts,

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:56.640
particularly in sickle cell,

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:59.880
and now they're expanding to
other types of blood diseases.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:02.430
Brio is a mental health platform

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:05.460
that uses community-led models of care,

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:06.870
and it's really innovative

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.600
because they are helping
to support mental health

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.700
and wellbeing, but are really taking in

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:15.120
the expertise of the local
communities where they work.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:18.240
So to date, InnovateHealth
Yale has supported

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:23.040
over 52 startups operating
in over 30 countries,

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:27.810
and we've given out about
$400,000 in impact grants.

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:29.100
And the really exciting thing

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:31.770
is that we've seen our alumni go on

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:35.310
to continue to be supported by Techstars,

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:38.280
the Chan Zuckerberg Initiative, Google,

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.574
and leading venture capital firms.

00:02:40.574 --> 00:02:43.741
(gentle upbeat music)

00:02:49.488 --> 00:02:52.505
(gentle upbeat music fades)

